+Preparation leaves the session and its stages untouched. A candidate binds to its parent
+stage, training data and fit by value, so it applies to any fork of the same fit; applying
+installs its exact working and inference banks without recomputing them. The identity
+candidate, `unchanged`, is the parent itself: applying it removes the requested stage and
+its descendants. Restoring a candidate validates all of its content, and the estimator
+built to validate it becomes its result's model. Candidate bytes can only be restored
+against their matching parent; they are separate from ordinary inference exports and
+resumable training checkpoints.
+
+`PrunePath(selection, budgets)` prunes at total-table budgets, each with the selection's
+path settings; by default the budgets are `pruning_budgets(selection)`.
+`BandPath(base, fractions)`: banding's six default positive budget fractions are 1/32,
+1/16, 1/8, 1/4, 1/2 and 1. Noise tolerance scales with the square root of that fraction
+and the deviance cap scales linearly, so no candidate exceeds `base`'s maxima. Numeric and
+categorical setup, master cuts and prefix sums are shared; each candidate calibrates
+independently.
+
+`SmoothPath(base, strengths)`: smoothing's default multipliers are 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2 and 4,
+plus `base.strength` if it differs. Table decompositions and pre-guard proposals are
+shared. Higher-order smoothing stays fixed at `base.high_order_alpha` for every positive
+point. A manual `base.alpha` follows the ordinary direct-solve policy, multiplied by each
+value. Reports distinguish proposed, requested and applied alpha; a global training guard
+rejection retains the original bank. EDF is measured before final constraints and
+reanchoring. These library diagnostics are training evidence; an application must
+evaluate retained-fit candidates on its own held-out rows before making validation claims.
